From: sluongng <26684313+sluongng@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] regex: Expose regex set search cache pool capacity

The meta engine already supports configuring cache-pool shard count, but the
regex set builder does not expose it. A regex used sequentially across many
threads can otherwise retain one cache per shard despite serialized callers.
Expose an additive builder passthrough for string and byte regex sets. Defaults
and engine selection remain unchanged. This controls shard count, not the size
of each cache or simultaneous-search admission; callers retain that ownership.
---
diff --git a/src/builders.rs b/src/builders.rs
--- a/src/builders.rs
+++ b/src/builders.rs
@@ -191,6 +191,11 @@
         self
     }
 
+    fn pool_capacity(&mut self, capacity: usize) -> &mut Builder {
+        self.metac = self.metac.clone().pool_capacity(capacity);
+        self
+    }
+
     fn nest_limit(&mut self, limit: u32) -> &mut Builder {
         self.syntaxc = self.syntaxc.nest_limit(limit);
         self
@@ -1311,6 +1316,18 @@
             self
         }
 
+        /// Set the number of cache-pool shards used by regex searches.
+        ///
+        /// This does not limit simultaneous searches or the size of each cache.
+        /// With one shard and externally serialized searches, the pool retains
+        /// at most the owner cache and one non-owner cache, independent of the
+        /// number of threads that sequentially use this regex set. Defaults
+        /// remain unchanged when this method is not called.
+        pub fn pool_capacity(&mut self, capacity: usize) -> &mut RegexSetBuilder {
+            self.builder.pool_capacity(capacity);
+            self
+        }
+
         /// Set the nesting limit for this parser.
         ///
         /// The nesting limit controls how deep the abstract syntax tree is
@@ -2495,6 +2512,18 @@
             self
         }
 
+        /// Set the number of cache-pool shards used by regex searches.
+        ///
+        /// This does not limit simultaneous searches or the size of each cache.
+        /// With one shard and externally serialized searches, the pool retains
+        /// at most the owner cache and one non-owner cache, independent of the
+        /// number of threads that sequentially use this regex set. Defaults
+        /// remain unchanged when this method is not called.
+        pub fn pool_capacity(&mut self, capacity: usize) -> &mut RegexSetBuilder {
+            self.builder.pool_capacity(capacity);
+            self
+        }
+
         /// Set the nesting limit for this parser.
         ///
         /// The nesting limit controls how deep the abstract syntax tree is
